<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl Polanyi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl Polanyi was an Austro-Hungarian economic historian and social theorist, best known for &quot;The Great Transformation&quot; (1944), which argued that the self-regulating market economy was a historical novelty. He held that earlier economies were embedded in social relations, and in &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a>&quot; (1966) used the West African kingdom as a case of a redistributive economy with administered trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-polanyi.jpg" alt="Karl Polanyi" width="294" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pol%C3%A1nyi_K%C3%A1roly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Karl Polanyi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 25 October 1886 and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> by a Jewish family that spoke German and was assimilating into the secular middle class. His father, Mihály Pollacsek, was a railway entrepreneur; his younger brother was the philosopher Michael Polanyi. He studied law at the University of Budapest, came under the influence of the legal philosopher Gyula Pikler, and in 1908 became the first president of the freethinking Galileo Circle. He took a doctorate in law at Kolozsvár in 1909 and was called to the bar in 1912. In June 1914 he helped Oszkár Jászi found the National Citizens' Radical Party and became its secretary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Polanyi served as a second lieutenant in the Austro-Hungarian cavalry on the Russian Front until he was hospitalised in 1917. He supported the republican government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihaly-karolyi">Mihály Károlyi</a>, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1919 and did not return after Admiral Horthy's regime took power. From 1924 to 1933 he was a senior editor of the magazine Der Österreichische Volkswirt, where he began to criticise the Austrian school of economics. He married the communist revolutionary Ilona Duczyńska, and their daughter Kari Polanyi Levitt also became an economist.</p><p>After Hitler's accession in January 1933 the magazine's liberal publisher could not keep a prominent socialist, and Polanyi moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He worked as a journalist and tutor and from 1936 as a lecturer for the Workers' Educational Association, but never found a post at a British university. In 1940 he joined Bennington College in Vermont, and from 1947 to 1953 he taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>. His wife could not obtain a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> visa because she had been a communist, so they lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and he commuted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He died at Pickering, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, in 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known book, The Great Transformation (1944), questions whether a self-regulating market is a sound idea. It describes the enclosure process in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the making of the modern economic system early in the 19th century, and holds that market societies in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> were not inevitable but historically contingent. He called the interaction of marketisation and the push for social protection against it the Double Movement.</p><p>Polanyi is remembered as the originator of substantivism, which stresses that economies are embedded in society and culture. With a grant from the Ford Foundation he studied the economies of ancient empires, and his Columbia seminar led to Trade and Market in the Early Empires (1957). His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a>: An Analysis of an Archaic Economy appeared in 1966. His approach has been applied to Pre-Columbian America and ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, though its usefulness for ancient societies in general has been questioned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl Polanyi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dahomey's centralised government, royal ceremonies and women soldiers fascinated European observers, and the anthropologist Melville Herskovits and the economic historian <b>Karl Polanyi</b> made it a classic case in debates about African states and economies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Karl Polanyi</b> disagreed, arguing that until a competitive labour market was established in England in 1834, industrial capitalism as a social system did not exist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Pickering, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1936" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Karl Polanyi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1936">What was here in 1936? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
